WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.562
Now let's run the first SELECT example.

00:00:02.562 --> 00:00:07.052
Before you select Run, read the query from top to bottom.

00:00:07.052 --> 00:00:13.832
Notice which columns are requested, which table supplies the data, and how the output is sorted.

00:00:13.832 --> 00:00:18.017
Run it directly in the browser and inspect the result table.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.802
Look at the column names and the row order.

00:00:20.802 --> 00:00:23.887
The query is not changing anything in the database.

00:00:23.887 --> 00:00:27.047
It is only creating a result from stored rows.

00:00:27.047 --> 00:00:30.872
Then make a connection between each clause and what you see.

00:00:30.872 --> 00:00:33.922
SELECT controls what appears as columns.

00:00:33.922 --> 00:00:36.547
FROM identifies the source table.

00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:40.917
ORDER BY changes the order in which the rows are displayed.

00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:46.122
If you want to practice in pgAdmin, copy the same query into the Query Tool and compare the

00:00:46.122 --> 00:00:52.912
result. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:52.912 --> 00:00:57.052
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:57.052 --> 00:01:01.342
and the shape of the expected output.

00:01:01.342 --> 00:01:07.112
In a real setting such as an analyst exploring operational records before building a report,

00:01:07.112 --> 00:01:12.322
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:15.207
before discussing syntax.

00:01:15.207 --> 00:01:21.405
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
